Kaspersky's own descriptions of the secondary tools differ from those given earlier: httpMalice is a PebbleDash-based backdoor that talks over HTTP or HTTPS in version 1.9 and through the Dropbox API in version 1.8, MemLoad is the loader that runs anti-VM checks and reconnaissance before reflectively loading httpTroy, and httpTroy is the backdoor for long-term access and data exfiltration ([Kaspersky Securelist, 2026-05-14](https://securelist.com/kimsuky-appleseed-pebbledash-campaigns/119785/)). The report gives observations rather than detection guidance; the detection described now follows the dropper, persistence and tunnel artifacts it documents ([Kaspersky Securelist, 2026-05-14](https://securelist.com/kimsuky-appleseed-pebbledash-campaigns/119785/)). HelloDoor is judged early-stage, and its TryCloudflare-hosted C2 makes its infrastructure difficult to trace; Kimsuky also tunnels through VSCode Tunneling and Ngrok ([Kaspersky Securelist, 2026-05-14](https://securelist.com/kimsuky-appleseed-pebbledash-campaigns/119785/)). Targets are mainly South Korean, AppleSeed most often government bodies and PebbleDash the defense, military and medical sectors, and PebbleDash victims also include Brazilian defense organizations and a German defense firm over several years ([Kaspersky Securelist, 2026-05-14](https://securelist.com/kimsuky-appleseed-pebbledash-campaigns/119785/)).
